\section{The shape governs how well this samples}{
Near the shift the Weibull density behaves like \code{(y - ndt)^(mu - 1)}, and
that exponent decides how the mixture behaves as \code{ndt} passes an observation.
Three regimes, in order of severity:
\itemize{
\item \code{mu < 1}: the density is \strong{unbounded at \code{ndt}}, so the likelihood is
unbounded as \code{ndt} approaches the fastest response. The outlier component
adds density rather than capping it, so it cannot repair that.
\item \code{mu < 2}: the density is bounded, but the derivative of the log-likelihood
with respect to \code{ndt} behaves like \code{(y - ndt)^(mu - 2)} and so is
\strong{unbounded at every observation}. The posterior is proper - \code{poutlier}
keeps it so - but the gradient spikes wherever \code{ndt} sits close to a
response, which is exactly where the data put it.
\item \code{mu > 2}: bounded gradient. \code{mu > 3} additionally bounds the curvature.
}

The middle regime is the one to watch, because nothing warns about it. On the
4285-trial lexical-decision data in \code{vignette("rt_models")} the shape comes
out at 1.4, \code{ndt} lands at 0.40 s inside the dense left edge of the data, and
the sampler's step size collapses to 0.005 against 0.19 for
\code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}} on the same data: mean treedepth 8.1 against 3.9, which
is 19x the gradient evaluations and 19x the wall time, with \code{Rhat} 1.18 on
\code{ndt}. The density itself is cheap; \strong{all} of the cost is geometry.
\subsection{What does not help}{

All of the obvious remedies were tried on that fit and measured. None of them
works, and two make it worse, so they are recorded here rather than left for
the next person to rediscover.

\strong{A prior on the shape.} \code{normal(2.4, 0.4)} on the softplus scale puts 95\%
of its mass above \code{mu = 1.9}. It moved the posterior shape by 0.01, because
the likelihood prefers the low-shape corner by around 100 log units and the
prior contributes 5.

\strong{A narrow prior on \code{ndt}.} This looks like the obvious fix - keep the shift
below the data and the singular region is never visited - and it fails for an
instructive reason. \code{normal(-1.25, 0.05)}, centred at 0.287 s with 95\% of its
mass below the fastest bulk response, left the posterior at 0.396 s: \strong{6.5
prior SDs away}, essentially where it was without any prior at all. The
\code{ndt} likelihood has a posterior SD of 0.003, so it is some fifteen times
sharper than that prior; nothing weaker than fixing \code{ndt} outright competes
with it. What the attempt did achieve was 4\% divergent transitions against
0.5\%, 16\% of iterations at maximum treedepth against 7\%, \code{Rhat} 1.43 against
1.18, and a slightly worse \code{loo}.

\strong{Fixing \code{ndt} at the fastest observed response.} This does remove the
problem, by removing the parameter - but it reinstates exactly the min-RT
bound this parameterization exists to get rid of, and it is unsound wherever
the outlier component is doing its job. On the data above the fastest
response is 71 ms, which is not a decision; the mixture is there precisely so
that an order statistic of the sample is not treated as a bound. See
\code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}}.

Note also what is \emph{not} wrong: \code{ndt} and the shape are jointly identified,
and sharply so - the posterior SD on \code{ndt} is 3 ms. This is not a case of two
parameters trading off with nothing to separate them, so pinning one of them
is not the missing ingredient. The sharpness simply sits on a ridge that is
not smooth.
}

\subsection{What to do instead}{

Treat a fitted shape below 2 as the diagnostic it is, and use
\code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}}, which nests this family at \code{shape = 1} and lets the data
choose the shape rather than having the family fix it. On the data above it
samples in a third of the time with no divergences.

The slow sampling and the poor fit are the same fact, not two problems.
Across the ten families fitted in \code{vignette("rt_models")} the Weibull comes
\strong{last} by \code{loo}, 196 elpd (SE 21) behind \code{\link[=cogmod_loggamma]{cogmod_loggamma()}} and 95 behind
the next worst. What the sampler struggles with is the model contorting
itself - pushing the shift up into the data, pulling the shape toward 1 - to
represent a left edge it cannot otherwise reach. That does not make the
Weibull useless for reaction times in general; where the shape comes out
above 2 the family is perfectly well behaved, as \code{\link[=cogmod_gamma]{cogmod_gamma()}} is on these
same data at a shape of 2.2. It does mean a shape below 2 should be read as
the model telling you to use a different one.

Under the older \code{ndt = tau * min(RT)} parameterization the problem was hidden
rather than absent: the logit Jacobian vanished as \code{tau} approached 1, which
damped exactly this gradient.
}
}

\section{Starting values}{
Do \strong{not} fit this with \code{init = 0}: it puts \code{ndt} at \code{exp(0) = 1} second and
the shape at \code{softplus(0) = 0.69}, inside the \code{mu < 1} regime above, and no
single scalar avoids both. Use \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}}, which sets them separately:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{brms::brm(f, data = df, prior = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f), init = cogmod_inits(f, df))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

See \code{\link[=cogmod_inits]{cogmod_inits()}} for why, and \code{?rcogmod_gamma} for what it costs when
ignored.
}

